\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$ and $\mathbb T=\partial\mathbb D$. The Dirichlet space $\mathcal D$ consists of analytic functions on $\mathbb D$ with \[\|h\|_{\mathcal D}^2=|h(0)|^2+\int_{\mathbb D}|h^{\prime}(z)|^2\,dA(z)<\infty.\] For $h\in\mathcal D$, let $\mathcal Ph=\{ph:p\in\mathbb C[z]\}$, and say $h$ is cyclic if its closure in this norm is all of $\mathcal D$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
If $g\in\mathcal D$ is cyclic, $f\in\mathcal D$, and $|f(z)|\ge|g(z)|$ for every $z\in\mathbb D$, must $f$ also be cyclic? \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does pointwise modulus domination of a cyclic Dirichlet function preserve cyclicity?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2308011 (AMR-022-8011). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Walter K. Hayman and Edward F. Lingham, \emph{Research Problems in Function Theory} (2018), arXiv:1809.07200. Problem 8.11, complete original question, all following updates, and the relevant chapter definitions. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
